  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  version: \"0.2.2.0\"\n\npackage:\n  name: lgpio\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://pypi.org/packages/source/l/lgpio/lgpio-${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 11372e653b200f76a0b3ef8a23a0735c85ec678a9f8550b9893151ed0f863fff\n\nbuild:\n  number: 1\n  # Linux SBC GPIO library; only meaningful on Linux.\n  skip: not linux\n  # PYPI=1 builds a statically linked module bundling liblgpio (no system liblgpio needed).\n  script:\n    env:\n      PYPI: \"1\"\n    content:\n      - ${{ PYTHON }} -m pip install . -vv --no-deps --no-build-isolation\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('c') }}\n    - swig\n  host:\n    - python\n    - pip\n    - setuptools\n  run:\n    - python\n\ntests:\n  - python:\n      imports:\n        - lgpio\n      pip_check: true\n\nabout:\n  summary: Linux SBC GPIO module\n  description: |\n    lgpio provides Python bindings for the lg library to control the GPIO\n    of Linux single-board computers such as the Raspberry Pi through the\n    /dev/gpiochip character devices. It supports reading and writing GPIO\n    lines, alerts/callbacks on level changes, software PWM and waves, as\n    well as I2C, SPI, and serial communication. This build statically\n    links liblgpio, so no system lg installation is required.\n  license: Unlicense\n  license_file: LICENSE\n  homepage: http://abyz.me.uk/lg/py_lgpio.html\n  repository: https://github.com/joan2937/lg\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - ruben-arts\n",
